.TH SLOWSCROLL 1 "October 2026" "slowscroll 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slowscroll \- touchpad scroll speed, acceleration and momentum for libinput
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slowscroll
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIEVDEV\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-curve\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
libinput has no setting for two\-finger scroll speed. \fBslowscroll\fR grabs the
touchpad, re\-emits its events through a virtual touchpad, and scales the
shared movement of two or three fingers. Two\-finger scrolling follows a smooth
speed\-dependent acceleration curve and ends with a unified momentum glide, so
every application scrolls the same way. The compositor and applications see an
ordinary touchpad.
.PP
It is normally run as a systemd user service:
.PP
.RS
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now slowscroll.service
.RE
.PP
The settings can be changed with the \fBslowscroll\-settings\fR(1) window or by
editing the configuration file; changes are applied within a second.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
KEY=VALUE settings file, re\-read when it changes (default:
\fI~/.config/slowscroll.conf\fR). See \fI/usr/share/doc/slowscroll/slowscroll.conf.example\fR
for all keys.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIEVDEV\fR
Use this evdev node instead of auto\-detecting the touchpad.
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
List detected touchpads and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-curve\fR
Print the acceleration curve for the current settings and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/slowscroll.conf
User settings.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70\-slowscroll.rules
Grants the user at the console access to touchpads and /dev/uinput.
.SH SECURITY
Access to /dev/uinput allows a process to create virtual input devices,
including keyboards. The udev rule gives this to the user at the console
(the uaccess tag). Remove \fI70\-slowscroll.rules\fR if that is not acceptable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libinput (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Osama Algendy
